Local kernel patches
====================

Patches in this directory apply on top of the pinned Perpetua commit
(scripts/fetch-perpetua.sh), in lexical order, with `git apply --index`, and
the build fails loudly when one stops applying.

A kernel patch is licensed like the file it changes (the kernel's own rule,
Documentation/process/license-rules.rst): the driver and sound patches here
modify GPL-2.0-only files and are GPL-2.0-only (LICENSES/GPL-2.0-only.txt);
the device-tree patches (0001, 0003, 0005, 0022–0024, 0026, 0030, 0031, 0038, 0061) modify files that
carry `SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause` — Qualcomm's `kaanapali.dtsi` and
the Denial project's board file `kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts` (map220v) —
and are BSD-3-Clause; the two binding patches (0056, 0059) modify Documentation/devicetree/bindings schemas, which the kernel dual-licenses `(GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)` — 0059's new `maxic,mt5806.yaml` declares the same — and carry that dual license. Authorship is the `From:` line of each patch; the
`Signed-off-by:` lines are the Developer Certificate of Origin (DCO 1.1) of
the people who wrote or carried it, which is what the kernel and Perpetua
require instead of a contributor agreement.

Policy, inherited from both parent projects:

- The first response to a missing feature is a patch *to Perpetua upstream*
  (github.com/denialwm/linux-perpetua), not a file here. This directory is
  for what upstream has not merged yet, carried with its submission status
  (docs/upstreaming.md), and for genuinely Ubuntu-specific needs upstream
  would rightly reject.
- Never a fix imported from a vendor kernel because it exists there; the
  device's downstream kernel is a reference for what the hardware needs,
  never a base (Perpetua devices/README states the same rule).
- Every patch names its origin and its exit condition in its header: merged
  upstream (drop on the next pin bump) or permanent (say why).

