| 0074–0076 | this port: the PD PPS B-series on the SuperVOOC session driver — 0074 moves the adapter-independent policy arithmetic (derates, taper, entry ladder, 55 W budget, the gauge-referenced running window, the second-pump rules, admissibility) into sc8547_direct_policy.c as pure functions with a KUnit suite (15 cases pinning every number of plan §3.2; a hidden tristate selected by the driver, as oplus_svooc is); 0075 separates the session from the VOOC exchange behind struct svooc_adapter_ops (setup, start, tick, busy, change, stop, restore; the quiet window, the restore condition and the pre-grant rule as per-back-end fields) with the VOOC exchange as the first instance; 0076 refuses system suspend while a session runs (−EBUSY in IDENTIFY..STOPPING, an "asleep" mark the worker honours, cancel_delayed_work_sync before the state read, resume re-kicks the work). No behaviour change in 0074/0075 by construction: the generated code's order of checks and operands is the base's, argued line by line in the implementer's notes (build/pps-work/b-series) | John Stockdale | written 2026-11-03 by the implementation workflow against plan revision 2, three adversarial reviews taken (an unlocked state write and a suspend/worker race found and fixed before entry), a second five-reviewer pass over the result; KUnit 19/19 on the host under UML (tools/testing/kunit/kunit.py, the port's first KUnit use); both modules build W=1 clean; not yet flown — the brick stages run on the shipped #46 module (plan §11 rule 3) and the refactored pair (sc8547_direct_policy.ko then sc8547_svooc.ko, same build) flies as the B-gate before any bundle carries it |
| 0077 | this port: the SuperVOOC session driver keeps every CPU out of its deep idle states for the session — `cpu_latency_qos_add_request(0)` when the session enters IDENTIFY, removed when the buck input is restored and in the cleanup path — as the vendor AP VOOC PHY driver does for the whole fast-charge session (oplus_voocphy_pm_qos_update, cpu_latency_qos 0). The fix for the two silent resets of 2026-11-03: the factory adapter, asked above its 55 W class, steps its output down 300 s after granting fast charge, and the pumps' interrupts and I2C transfers at that transient landed on a cluster free to power down (Denial's lockup trace of the same freezes: both pumps' I2C timing out, then the charger firmware, RPMh and two CPUs). The vendor's frequency floors are left out (the reply took ≤ 1.8 ms without them) | John Stockdale | written 2026-11-04 after runs A and A2 (the states disabled through sysfs) survived the step; checkpatch 0/0/0; **flown as flight D1 2026-11-04 10:13 on #46 as a module swap with the policy library**: the brick's +300 s step crossed at 46 W with cpuidle in the governor's hands, the deep-idle usage sum flat for the whole session, 0 error frames; entered the series the same day. The number had been reserved for the PD PPS back-end, which takes 0084/0085; 0078–0083 (the conformance review's fixes: adapter class cap, hysteresis, stray frames, the change path, the second pump, register fidelity) are written and staged under build/pps-work/d-series and enter after their flights |

Numbering note: the number 0029 was first used on 2026-11-02 for infiniti's
ADSP reserved-memory change (0x4c00000 -> 0x5000000) and withdrawn the same
day — the OnePlus board file already deletes and redefines that node with
0x5000000, so it had no effect here (docs/porting-log.md, "0029 was a
no-op"). The number was then reused for the iris platform data.

Pin bump 2026-11-03 (rung P): 7c98a10e6bb7 → b7d639cfb282
-----------------------------------------------------------

The whole series was replayed with `git am -3` on Perpetua perpetua/v7.3 =
b7d639cfb282 (100 commits past the old pin) on 2026-11-02; 56 patches applied
untouched and four were re-diffed, replacing the same-numbered files here
(the interim copies lived in a p-pinbump/ directory until 2026-11-03, when
they were byte-identical to the series and the directory was removed):

- 0028: the READ_DONE half is upstream (9ba37ef1db70); the WRITE_DONE half
  is kept.
- 0031: the head moved the CPU thermal overrides into a shared include; the
  patch is now just the &iris block.
- 0058: the head added Xiaomi's pmic-glink match data next to ours; both kept. The re-diff kept the hBoost client keyed on "oplus,kaanapali-pmic-glink" although Perpetua 5ff346c547a7, before the pin, had renamed the board's compatible to "oneplus,kaanapali-pmic-glink" (generic fallback kept): on #45 the generic entry matched, no pmic_glink.hboost device was created and the pen charger read chip_id 0x0000 — found by the checkpoint audit on 2026-11-03 and corrected the same day (0058 now matches the renamed string; pmic_glink is built in, so the fix flies with build #46).
- 0061: the head added the SuperVOOC nodes (i2c_hub_0 charge pumps, PMK8850
  ADC channels, tlmm pump IRQ states) at the same insertion points as the
  pen charger's; both kept (i2c_hub_1, PMIC-GPIO states, gpio169 state).

0020 was retired at the same time: at the new pin Perpetua's TFA9865 driver
puts the two middle amplifiers in stock's playing state by itself (registers
5f:00e0 and 76:d700 read back on build #45), so the local patch had nothing
left to do. The number is not reused. The config fragments were re-merged
against the head's devices/iceland/kernel.config (ADC5 Gen3/Gen4,
CHARGER_SC8547, CHARGER_SC8547_SVOOC, oplus_svooc, and the lockup detectors
Denial added after two freezes during SuperVOOC). Build #45 (kernel 7.3.0+)
was RAM-booted and then flashed to boot_b on 2026-11-03.

Numbering: the ath12k regulatory series took 0064–0067 and the 11d-scan patch 0068 on 2026-11-03 (first flown as module swaps on #45/#46). The input-identity patch for the pen and touch devices was first
filed as 0043 on 2026-11-03, colliding with the first infiniti iris fix; it is
0063 since the same day (the series applies in lexical order, and it touches
only nt36xxx.c, which no later patch changes). It was written after build #45 had started: #45 applied 61 patch files and carries 0063's change in its nt36536 module, compiled by hand; the next build takes it from the series.
